  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1031 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nA chilly start to the weekend as high pressure is now in control \nover the FA with tranquil conditions. Highs today will only be in the \n50s with lows progged near 32 degrees. While temperatures on the NBM \nare below the 50th percentile Sunday morning, MOS guidance again is \nrunning a few degrees warmer. Unlike this morning, tonight the \nentire FA will experience mostly clear skies that will allow the \nboundary layer to quickly decouple with calm winds. Given robust \nradiational cooling in a dry airmass, tend to lean towards the \ncolder NBM which means a widespread freeze that will end the growing \nseason for most of the region. For this reason, a Freeze Warning \nremains in effect tonight. Patchy fog will also be possible near \nwarm bodies of water. \n\nThe colder temperatures do not last long as southerly return flow on \nSunday begins to advect in warmer air with a modest increase in \nmoisture. More widespread patchy fog along with frost Monday morning \nis favored. High temperatures Monday through Wednesday will be above \nnormal in the upper 60s to near 70 degrees before a potent cold \nfront plows through the FA late Wednesday. The NBM continues to \ntrend wetter with the LREF now supporting a 30-50% chance of rain \nshowers Wednesday afternoon into Wednesday evening, exceeding a \ntenth or two of an inch. However, the deterministic GFS remains more \nrobust with a low pressure system rapidly developing over western \nTennessee with heavy rainfall and strong gusty winds persisting into \nThursday. The GFS at the moment is a large outlier as the left \nentrance region associated with a 130 kt jet at 250 mb would favor \nthe more progressive AI GFS/EC/EC AIFS that would only bring light \nprecipitation to the FA, supported by the LREF.\n\nPutting it all together, after scattered rain showers exit the \nregion by Thursday morning, lingering sprinkles will remain possible \non Thursday with breezy conditions. An amplified broad 500 mb trough \nand embedded vort max with 700 mb moisture will dig across the FA. \nDue to a tight pressure gradient with increasing cyclogenesis \nfavored more east over the Mid Atlantic region, wind gusts over 20 \nmph will be possible during the day with northwest flow. In fact, \n850 mb temps plummeting below 0C will translate to a 40-60% chance \nof high temperatures struggling to reach 50 degrees on Friday as the \ncoldest airmass of the season so far will persist into next\nweekend.",
